---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";

/* Sourced from docs/notes/competitor-facts-2026-08-15.md, section 5, and
 * nothing else. That section was added after this page was first refused: the
 * facts did not exist, so the page was not written, which is the only correct
 * order for these.
 *
 * THE OVERCLAIM WARNING APPLIES IN FULL. SE.Live SELECTS an OBS audio track per
 * platform output and cannot sum; the summing happened upstream in Advanced
 * Audio Properties. Same case as obs-multi-rtmp and Aitum. "It cannot do
 * per-destination audio" is false and is not written here.
 *
 * WHICH IS EXACTLY WHY THE AUDIO SECTION ON THIS PAGE IS SHORT AND LINKS OUT.
 * The argument has been made at length twice already. Making it a third time
 * would be the same page published again -- thin to a crawler, duplicated to
 * SonarCloud, and boring to the one reader who has clicked all three. So this
 * page states the fact once, sends anyone who wants it argued to the pages that
 * argue it, and spends its own length on the two things that are true of
 * SE.Live and of nothing else on this site:
 *
 *   1. Windows only. macOS and Linux are not supported. No other tool compared
 *      anywhere on this site has a hard platform stop.
 *   2. RTMP/RTMPS egress only, and no ingest at all -- it is not a relay. SRT is
 *      not supported, multitrack SRT least of all. On the other plugin pages the
 *      ingest question was a footnote; here it settles the matter outright,
 *      because a multitrack workflow cannot start.
 *
 * The disambiguation is also NOT the one on /vs/restreamer, and the difference
 * matters. There, two unrelated companies shipped confusingly similar names.
 * Here one company ships two things, and the famous one -- the overlays and
 * chat dashboard everybody has heard of -- does not multistream video at all.
 */
const CHECKED = "15 August 2026";

/* A verdict per product rather than a feature card, because the question a
 * reader actually arrives with is "does the StreamElements I already use do
 * this", and that is answerable yes or no before any comparing starts. */
const whichOne = [
  {
    name: "StreamElements Cloud",
    what: "The web dashboard. Unified multi-platform chat, activity feeds, overlays and tipping.",
    verdict: "No video multistreaming",
    ok: false,
    then: "If this is the StreamElements you use, it does not restream video anywhere, and nothing on this page is a comparison with it.",
  },
  {
    name: "SE.Live",
    what: "Formerly OBS.Live. A plugin that installs into OBS Studio on your own machine.",
    verdict: "This is the multistreaming one",
    ok: true,
    then: "Everything below is about this. It is free, and it is the only video multistreaming StreamElements ships.",
  },
];

const merits = [
  {
    h: "No cap on destinations, and no paid tier",
    p: "Free at $0 with no paywall above it, and no ceiling on how many platforms you publish to — the limit is your encoding capacity and your upstream bandwidth, which is an honest limit rather than a billing one.",
  },
  {
    h: "Canvas does vertical and horizontal at once",
    p: "Its Canvas system produces simultaneous 16:9 and 9:16 output from a single OBS session, with Twitch Dual-Output Beta wired in. polyemesis has no equivalent and this page is not going to pretend otherwise — if a vertical cut of your broadcast matters, that is a reason to run SE.Live.",
  },
  {
    h: "Per-destination encoding, with aspect ratio in the mix",
    p: "Shared copy or an independent re-encode per platform, each with its own resolution, aspect, bitrate, framerate and encoder. Those re-encodes run concurrently on the local machine.",
  },
  {
    h: "GPL-2.0 core, actively supported",
    p: "The core plugin is GPL-2.0 and openly published; the cloud backend it connects to is proprietary. Nothing about it was deprecated in the year to this check.",
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="StreamElements multistream: SE.Live vs polyemesis"
  description="StreamElements multistreaming means SE.Live, a Windows-only OBS plugin. It selects an OBS audio track per platform and sends no SRT at all."
  path="/vs/streamelements"
>
  <Breadcrumbs
    crumbs={[
      { label: "polyemesis", href: "/" },
      { label: "Comparison", href: "/comparison" },
      { label: "StreamElements", },
    ]}
  />

  <section class="wrap py-12 sm:py-16">
    <p class="eyebrow">Compared</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 max-w-3xl text-balance text-4xl font-bold leading-tight sm:text-5xl">
      StreamElements multistreaming is SE.Live. It runs on Windows only.
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-pretty text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted">
      Anyone looking into StreamElements multistream support meets a fork in the
      road immediately, and it is not signposted well: the StreamElements almost
      everybody knows — overlays, tips, the chat dashboard — sends no video
      anywhere. The multistreaming lives in a separate OBS plugin called SE.Live,
      and before comparing anything it is worth knowing which one you are holding.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-8 max-w-3xl rounded-token border-l-2 border-primary bg-surface/60 py-4 pl-5 pr-4">
      <p class="text-pretty leading-relaxed text-fg">
        <strong>Two facts settle most of this page:</strong> SE.Live is supported
        on Windows and not on macOS or Linux, and it publishes over RTMP and
        RTMPS with no SRT support of any kind. The first rules it out for a lot
        of people outright. The second means the multitrack workflow polyemesis
        is built around cannot begin.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ---------------------------------------------------- which one is it --- */}
  <section class="border-t border-line py-16" data-reveal>
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-medium">Which StreamElements do you have?</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
        One company, two products, and only one of them touches video.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-8 max-w-4xl space-y-4">
        {
          whichOne.map((w) => (
            <div class="card p-6">
              <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-x-4 gap-y-2">
                <p class="font-medium text-fg">{w.name}</p>
                <p
                  class:list={[
                    "font-mono text-[11px]",
                    w.ok ? "text-live" : "text-subtle",
                  ]}
                >
                  {w.verdict}
                </p>
              </div>
              <p class="mt-3 text-pretty text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">{w.what}</p>
              <p class="mt-3 border-t border-line pt-3 text-pretty text-sm leading-relaxed text-subtle">
                {w.then}
              </p>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ------------------------------------------------------- the two stops -- */}
  <section class="border-t border-line bg-surface/30 py-16" data-reveal>
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-medium">Two limits worth checking before anything else</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
        Neither is a matter of degree, which is why they come first. Checked
        against StreamElements' own documentation on
        <span class="font-mono text-[13px] text-fg">{CHECKED}</span>.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-8 grid max-w-5xl gap-5 lg:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="card p-6">
          <p class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-wider text-subtle">
            Operating system
          </p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-pretty leading-relaxed text-fg">
            Windows, and only Windows. macOS and Linux are not supported.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-pretty text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
            Nothing else compared on this site has a stop like this — the other
            plugins follow OBS wherever it runs, and the servers are containers.
            If you broadcast from a Mac, the comparison ends at this paragraph.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card p-6">
          <p class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-wider text-subtle">
            Transport
          </p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-pretty leading-relaxed text-fg">
            RTMP and RTMPS out. No SRT, and no ingest of any kind — it captures
            from the OBS it lives inside and is not a relay.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-pretty text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
            This is the cleanest version of the difference on any of these pages.
            polyemesis works by receiving your separated audio tracks over
            multitrack SRT and deciding what each platform hears from them.
            SE.Live has no way to send that, so the question of what to do with
            those tracks never arises.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ------------------------------------------------------------ the audio -- */}
  <section class="border-t border-line py-16" data-reveal>
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-medium">On the audio, it is the same story as the other two plugins</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-pretty leading-relaxed text-muted">
        SE.Live routes one OBS audio track — any of the six — to each platform
        output. So yes, it can give Twitch and YouTube different sound, and a
        comparison claiming otherwise would be wrong. What it cannot do is
        combine tracks into a new mix at the output; whatever those six tracks
        contain was decided earlier, in OBS's Advanced Audio Properties.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-pretty leading-relaxed text-muted">
        That is precisely the model obs-multi-rtmp and Aitum Multistream use, and
        it is argued at length on both of their pages rather than repeated here.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-sm text-subtle">
        The same argument, in full:
        <a href="/vs/aitum-multistream" class="text-primary underline-offset-4 hover:underline">Aitum Multistream</a>
        ·
        <a href="/vs/obs-multi-rtmp" class="text-primary underline-offset-4 hover:underline">obs-multi-rtmp</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ---------------------------------------------------------- their merits -- */}
  <section class="border-t border-line bg-surface/30 py-16" data-reveal>
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-medium">What SE.Live does well</h2>
      <div class="mt-8 grid gap-5 sm:grid-cols-2">
        {
          merits.map((m) => (
            <div class="card p-6">
              <p class="font-medium text-fg">{m.h}</p>
              <p class="mt-2 text-pretty text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">{m.p}</p>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-line py-16" data-reveal>
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-medium">Use SE.Live, not this</h2>
      <ul class="mt-5 max-w-3xl space-y-3">
        {
          [
            "You broadcast from Windows and one of the six OBS tracks already carries the right sound for each platform.",
            "You need a vertical output alongside the horizontal one. Canvas does that in a single session and polyemesis does not do it at all.",
            "You are already inside the StreamElements ecosystem for overlays, tips and chat, and adding a separate server to the setup buys you nothing you asked for.",
            "You want more destinations than a subscription would let you have, at no cost, and your machine can encode them.",
          ].map((l) => (
            <li class="flex gap-3 text-pretty leading-relaxed text-muted">
              <span class="mt-2 h-1 w-1 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-subtle" aria-hidden="true" />
              {l}
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-pretty leading-relaxed text-muted">
        polyemesis is the answer to a narrower question: you are not on Windows,
        or you want a mix assembled per platform rather than chosen from what OBS
        prepared, or you want the fan-out to leave a server instead of your own
        connection.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap py-20 text-center" data-reveal>
    <h2 class="text-balance text-3xl font-medium">Weighing up the others too?</h2>
    <div class="mt-7 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-3">
      <a href="/comparison" class="btn btn-ghost">All five compared</a>
      <a href="/download" class="btn btn-primary">Install polyemesis</a>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-subtle">
      Also compared:
      <a href="/vs/restreamer" class="text-primary underline-offset-4 hover:underline">Restreamer and restream.io</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
